WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar, Emmy,

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.370
and Tony-winning actor
you know from movies like

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.439
"Reversal of Fortune"
and "The Lion King"

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.908
as well as shows like
"Brideshead Revisited"

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.009
and "Watchmen."

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.877
He stars in
"Munich: The Edge of War,"

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.112
which is in theaters now

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.615
and available on Netflix
January 21st.

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:16.583
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:19.952
-Every time I...

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:23.389
pass a war memorial or...

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:26.259
visit one of those
vast cemeteries in France

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.261
where so many
of my friends lie buried,

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:31.864
I vow that if I find myself
in the position

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:34.200
where I can prevent
such a catastrophe

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.338
from happening again,
I shall do anything...

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:41.641
sacrifice anything.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:43.710
-Please welcome back to the show
Jeremy Irons.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.812
How are you, Jeremy?
-I'm good, Seth.

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:48.581
Very nice to see you.

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.116
-It's wonderful to see you, too.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.218
I will admit,
I was holding out hope

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:55.889
that you would be Zooming with
us from your castle in Ireland.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.991
We discussed this
last time you were on.

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:59.459
I hope you don't mind.
I don't know many people

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.027
with castles,
so I hope you don't mind.

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.696
-I'm sorry, Seth.
Yeah, I would have --

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.099
I would've gone over there,
but -- but, no,

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:10.236
I'm at an unknown location
in London,

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.105
separated from you
by a lot of water, sadly.

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:15.241
I'd like to be there with you

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:18.111
to get the full impact
of that smile.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.613
-Thank you.
-But, no, I'm here.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:23.482
-I know it does wane --
my smile does wane a little bit

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:25.451
over Zoom,
but it's in full force.

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:28.154
-No, it's good.
-For those who don't know,

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.656
these are beautiful photos
of your castle in Ireland

00:01:30.656 --> 00:01:32.992
that were in "Vanity Fair,"
and I do hope this is

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.995
a real dog of yours
and not just a prop dog.

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:38.130
-It is a real dog.
In fact, she's here.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.967
Somewhere.
Yeah, she's outside

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.169
looking at the foxes
or trying to catch a fox.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.871
-Oh, that's why. That would be
what I would want them to do.

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:48.374
Now, this is
a very exciting development.

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.676
Jeremy, let me be clear.
For most people,

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:53.279
I would be very jealous
that they have their own castle,

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:56.382
but I'm very happy
that you have your own.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:57.850
-Thank you, Seth.
-And now you have also bought

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.387
an island near the castle.
-How do you know that?

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.289
[ Laughter ]
-I was on --

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.825
I was on Zillow for islands.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.960
-All right.
Why, yes, I have.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.197
A very small island
with a little cottage on it.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.767
It's -- I can just about
swim there from the castle.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.169
It's about 100 meters.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:20.272
-I would say "just about"
is the worst possible distance.

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:23.643
-Well, yeah, I --
Yes, I can --

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.245
Well, my wife can swim there.
-Okay.

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:28.247
As long as there's
someone to throw you a line

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:29.982
from "close enough."
-Yeah.

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:33.085
-And are you going to put
livestock on your island?

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:36.222
-I am.
I'm gonna put some Kerry ponies

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:39.859
and some Galloway cattle
just to sort of eat it down.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:43.863
So I'm gonna -- I'm farming
a little bit more now

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.899
than acting, but...

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:50.202
-As long as you find time
for both, we're very satisfied.

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:52.138
-Yeah.
By the way, I have --

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:55.140
I've just finished work
with your brother, with Mike.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.410
-Oh. Well,
Mike is not my brother, but...

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.612
-No, he is.
He said he was.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:02.481
-He did say he was?
-He was, he did. He did.

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:04.317
When he rang me up and asked me
to do his show --

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:07.754
it's called "Pentaverate" --
he said, "I'm Seth's brother."

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.389
I said, "Oh, hello, Mike.
Yeah, right."

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.892
And that was why I did it,
because I thought he was your --

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:15.295
But he's not?
-No, he's a distant brother.

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:16.996
[ Laughter ]
-Is he?

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.265
-Twice removed.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.900
-Well, that's the only reason
I did it.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.371
-Well, it's too late now, sir.
-All right. Okay, okay.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.408
You look very alike.
-Yeah, we're very similar.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:29.608
-Yeah.
-Very similar.

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:31.210
Now, you --
I want to ask about --

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.578
Here's my final question --

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:34.113
I promise, this is my final
question about the castle.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:37.250
But I imagine you have --
there's a lot of upkeep.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.118
You deal with a lot of
local tradespeople.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.185
-I do.
-Are they aware

00:03:40.185 --> 00:03:42.521
that you are an actor?

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:43.890
Are they aware of your work?
-They are.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.024
-Okay.
-They are.

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:47.493
I remember
when we were building it,

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.262
we built the castellation
on the top.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.397
That's the bit that
goes up and down, you know?

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:55.101
And we --
we carefully planned it,

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:57.002
and we'd done models
and all of that.

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:58.804
And when I looked at it,
when it was finally built,

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:00.939
and I looked at it,
I thought it was wrong.

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:04.644
It's too small.
So I --

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:06.546
One of my friends said, "Well,
I'm not telling the builder.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.015
You go up there."
So I climbed up 13 floors

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:10.249
of scaffolding,
and I got to the top,

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.185
and I said,
"This is wonderful, guys.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:13.553
This is wonderful
what you've built,

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:16.021
but actually take it down.
We've got to make it bigger,

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:19.025
'cause it's not strong enough,
it doesn't look strong enough."

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:21.260
And he looked at me and he said,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.764
"You know, the trouble
with working with bloody actors

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.432
are the bloody rehearsals."

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:30.369
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:33.272
But he did, he knocked it down,
and we built it again.

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:36.409
So they do know --
they do know I'm an actor.

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:39.645
They put up with it, though.
-Now, this is a wonderful film.

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.413
You play Neville Chamberlain,

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:42.448
Prime Minister
Neville Chamberlain,

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:43.916
during the Munich Conference.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.718
This is obviously a very famous,
historical moment,

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.120
and yet we see --
I feel like we see so many films

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.622
about Winston Churchill

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:52.691
and almost none
with Neville Chamberlain.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:54.660
-Yeah.
-This is perhaps a kinder

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.197
portrayal of him
and how he was strategizing

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.233
about dealing with Hitler
than we've seen before.

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:01.534
Did that make it
more interesting,

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.803
that it was a different
Neville Chamberlain?

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:06.204
-Yeah, it did.
You see, we -- we know about

00:05:06.204 --> 00:05:09.041
Neville Chamberlain because of
what Churchill's written.

00:05:09.041 --> 00:05:11.043
Churchill wrote the history
of the war,

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:14.880
and he and Neville were --

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.284
They were competitors,
politically.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:23.923
And Churchill always wanted
to go to war in '37, '38.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:25.524
Chamberlain knew
we weren't ready,

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:28.260
and so was trying to put it off,
put it off,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.563
trying to make peace,
trying to, you know...

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:33.666
After all, the First World War
had only been 20 years before.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.335
They didn't
want to go to war again.

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:40.305
So, as a result, Churchill
thought of him as a weak man,

00:05:40.305 --> 00:05:45.644
as an appeaser, as somebody
who was taken in by Hitler,

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:48.114
whereas, in fact,
he was a strategist,

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:51.884
and he gave us another year
so we could build up

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:56.022
more of an air force,
so we could discover radar

00:05:56.022 --> 00:06:00.793
during that year, which really
helped us win, with your help --

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.394
not yours personally,
but, you know...

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:04.830
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.299
Oh, thank you!
Jeremy, thank you.

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:09.968
-People your parents knew.
Get-up-and-go Americans

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.770
like Seth Meyers and Mike Myers,
probably.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.440
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:18.077
-So, really,
had he not appeased,

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:22.948
had he not
had the Munich Conference,

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:24.950
we would have gone to war
a year earlier.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:26.885
We probably
would have lost immediately

00:06:26.885 --> 00:06:29.588
because we had no real defenses
at that point.

00:06:29.588 --> 00:06:32.258
-Well, it's a fascinating story,
and when we come back,

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:34.961
I have some questions
about your past

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:36.228
and how you were almost --
-Where are you going?

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:38.197
-What's that?
-You said you would

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:39.665
come back from where.
Where are you going?

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:43.035
-Well, I miss --
I miss my brother,

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:44.570
and I want to call him.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.272
-Okay.
Okay.

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.274
-We'll be right back
with more from Jeremy Irons.

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:49.742
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

